      <p>For more background on the enhancement, see Additional resources.</p>
      <h2 id="compliance-operator-checkresult-custom-metadata-configure-rules_compliance-operator-checkresult-metadata" class="group scroll-mt-20">Adding custom labels and annotations to a Rule object<a href="#compliance-operator-checkresult-custom-metadata-configure-rules_compliance-operator-checkresult-metadata" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">You can attach custom labels and annotations to a <code>Rule</code> that Open Security Content Automation Protocol (OpenSCAP) evaluates, and verify that they are displayed on the aggregated <code>ComplianceCheckResult</code> after the next scan.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You have installed Compliance Operator 1.9.0 or later.</li>
      <li>You have access to the <code>openshift-compliance</code> namespace (or the namespace where your rules and scans run).</li>
      <li>You identified the <code>Rule</code> object name and the <code>ComplianceScan</code> that evaluates it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Add custom labels to the rule. Replace <code>&lt;rule_name&gt;</code> with your <code>Rule</code> object name, and adjust labels as needed with the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc label rule.compliance/<rule_name> \\\n  business-unit=payments \\\n  risk-tier=critical \\\n  -n openshift-compliance"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Add custom annotations with the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc annotate rule.compliance/<rule_name> \\\n  internal-id=SEC-4021 \\\n  exception-ticket=JIRA-123 \\\n  -n openshift-compliance"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Long or free-form values are better suited to annotations than labels because of Kubernetes label length limits.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Trigger a new scan or wait for the next scheduled run. For example, to request a rescan of an existing <code>ComplianceScan</code> named <code>ocp4-cis</code> use the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc annotate compliancescan ocp4-cis \\\n  compliance.openshift.io/rescan= \\\n  -n openshift-compliance"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      After the scan finishes, list <code>ComplianceCheckResult</code> objects that carry your label using the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get compliancecheckresults \\\n  -l business-unit=payments \\\n  -n openshift-compliance"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Confirm an annotation on a specific result with the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get compliancecheckresult <result_name> \\\n  -o jsonpath='{.metadata.annotations.internal-id}' \\\n  -n openshift-compliance"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;result_name&gt;</code> with the <code>ComplianceCheckResult</code> name for your rule.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Optional: Verify that a user annotation survives a <code>ProfileBundle</code> content image update using the following command.
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get rule.compliance/<rule_name> \\\n  -o jsonpath='{.metadata.annotations.exception-ticket}' \\\n  -n openshift-compliance"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>After the profile parser reconciles the <code>Rule</code>, your key should still be present.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="compliance-operator-checkresult-custom-metadata-configure-customrules_compliance-operator-checkresult-metadata" class="group scroll-mt-20">Adding custom labels and annotations to a CustomRule object<a href="#compliance-operator-checkresult-custom-metadata-configure-customrules_compliance-operator-checkresult-metadata" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">For platform checks implemented as a <code>CustomRule</code> object and evaluated by the Common Expression Language (CEL) scanner, define labels and annotations on the <code>CustomRule</code> metadata. The Compliance Operator copies non-reserved entries onto each generated <code>ComplianceCheckResult</code>.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You have installed Compliance Operator 1.9.0 or later.</li>
      <li>You use a <code>TailoredProfile</code> (or equivalent workflow) that enables your <code>CustomRule</code> for a CEL profile scan.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a <code>CustomRule</code> object that includes your metadata in <code>metadata.labels</code> and <code>metadata.annotations</code>. The following example defines a platform CEL check; replace names, expressions, and metadata with values appropriate for your environment:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: compliance.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: CustomRule\nmetadata:\n  name: check-pod-security-standard\n  namespace: openshift-compliance\n  labels:\n    break_severity: critical\n    weakness_score: \"9.5\"\n  annotations:\n    internal-id: SEC-5500\n    audit-contact: platform-security-team\nspec:\n  id: check-pod-security-standard\n  title: \"Ensure Pod Security Standards are enforced\"\n  severity: high\n  checkType: Platform\n  scannerType: CEL\n  expression: |\n    namespaces.items.all(ns,\n      has(ns.metadata.labels) &&\n      \"pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce\" in ns.metadata.labels\n    )\n  failureReason: \"One or more namespaces do not enforce Pod Security Standards\"\n  inputs:\n    - name: namespaces\n      kubernetesInputSpec:\n        apiVersion: v1\n        resource: namespaces"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the manifest by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f custom-rule.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Reference the <code>CustomRule</code> object from a <code>TailoredProfile</code>, for example:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: compliance.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: TailoredProfile\nmetadata:\n  name: custom-cel-profile\n  namespace: openshift-compliance\nspec:\n  title: \"Custom CEL profile\"\n  description: \"Profile with custom CEL rules\"\n  enableRules:\n    - name: check-pod-security-standard\n      rationale: \"Enforce pod security standards\"\n      kind: CustomRule"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>Bind the tailored profile with a <code>ScanSettingBinding</code> and run the scan using your normal workflow.</li>
      <li>
      After the scan completes, query results by a custom label by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get compliancecheckresults \\\n  -l break_severity=critical \\\n  -n openshift-compliance"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>Identify the <code>ComplianceCheckResult</code> name for your <code>CustomRule</code> object in the scan output, for example by listing results in the namespace and filtering by labels or name.</li>
